+# Python Project Constitution
+
+<!-- TODO(PROJECT_NAME): replace "Python Project" with the real project name once chosen. -->
 
 ## Core Principles
 
-### [PRINCIPLE_1_NAME]
-<!-- Example: I. Library-First -->
-[PRINCIPLE_1_DESCRIPTION]
-<!-- Example: Every feature starts as a standalone library; Libraries must be self-contained, independently testable, documented; Clear purpose required - no organizational-only libraries -->
+### I. Standard Library Only
+
+The runtime MUST depend on nothing beyond the Python standard library. No third-party package
+may be imported by, vendored into, or required to execute any shipped code path. Installing the
+project MUST require no package manager step beyond obtaining the source itself.
+
+Development and test tooling is the single, narrow exception: `pytest` (and nothing else by
+default) MAY be required to run the test suite, because Principle III depends on it. Any
+additional development dependency MUST be justified in writing and approved as an amendment.
+
+Rationale: a zero-dependency runtime removes supply-chain risk, version-conflict debugging, and
+install friction — costs that dominate the total effort of a small project and are paid forever,
+while the convenience a library buys is paid once.
+
+### II. Fail Loudly
+
+Invalid, missing, or malformed input MUST produce a clear, specific diagnostic message AND a
+failure signal appropriate to the interface: a non-zero exit status for command-line entry
+points, or an explicit error response or raised exception for library and service entry points.
+
+The following are prohibited:
+
+- Returning a plausible-looking result derived from input the code could not validate.
+- Substituting a default, zero value, empty collection, or `None` for input that was required.
+- Catching an exception and continuing without either re-raising or reporting the failure.
+- Diagnostics that name no cause (e.g. "error occurred") or that omit the offending value.
 
-### [PRINCIPLE_2_NAME]
-<!-- Example: II. CLI Interface -->
-[PRINCIPLE_2_DESCRIPTION]
-<!-- Example: Every library exposes functionality via CLI; Text in/out protocol: stdin/args → stdout, errors → stderr; Support JSON + human-readable formats -->
+Error messages MUST state what was expected and what was received. Errors MUST be written to
+stderr; normal results MUST be written to stdout.
 
-### [PRINCIPLE_3_NAME]
-<!-- Example: III. Test-First (NON-NEGOTIABLE) -->
-[PRINCIPLE_3_DESCRIPTION]
-<!-- Example: TDD mandatory: Tests written → User approved → Tests fail → Then implement; Red-Green-Refactor cycle strictly enforced -->
+Rationale: a silent wrong answer is the most expensive defect class, because it is trusted and
+propagates. A crash is cheap by comparison — it is discovered immediately, at its own source.
 
-### [PRINCIPLE_4_NAME]
-<!-- Example: IV. Integration Testing -->
-[PRINCIPLE_4_DESCRIPTION]
-<!-- Example: Focus areas requiring integration tests: New library contract tests, Contract changes, Inter-service communication, Shared schemas -->
+### III. Every Requirement Is Testable
 
-### [PRINCIPLE_5_NAME]
-<!-- Example: V. Observability, VI. Versioning & Breaking Changes, VII. Simplicity -->
-[PRINCIPLE_5_DESCRIPTION]
-<!-- Example: Text I/O ensures debuggability; Structured logging required; Or: MAJOR.MINOR.BUILD format; Or: Start simple, YAGNI principles -->
+Every functional requirement in a specification MUST map to at least one automated `pytest`
+test. The mapping MUST be explicit: the test name, or a comment or docstring inside the test,
+MUST identify the requirement it covers.
 
-## [SECTION_2_NAME]
-<!-- Example: Additional Constraints, Security Requirements, Performance Standards, etc. -->
+- A requirement that cannot be expressed as a passing/failing automated test MUST be rewritten
+  until it can be, or removed from the specification.
+- A requirement with no corresponding test is incomplete work, regardless of whether the code
+  exists and appears to function.
+- Error paths required by Principle II MUST be tested, not only success paths.
+- The full suite MUST pass before any change is considered done.
 
-[SECTION_2_CONTENT]
-<!-- Example: Technology stack requirements, compliance standards, deployment policies, etc. -->
+Rationale: an untestable requirement cannot be verified or defended against regression, so it
+is an intention rather than a commitment. Requiring the mapping up front turns vague wording
+into a checkable contract while it is still cheap to fix.
 
-## [SECTION_3_NAME]
-<!-- Example: Development Workflow, Review Process, Quality Gates, etc. -->
+### IV. Simplicity
 
-[SECTION_3_CONTENT]
-<!-- Example: Code review requirements, testing gates, deployment approval process, etc. -->
+The implementation MUST be the smallest design that satisfies the specification. When two
+designs both meet the specification, the one with fewer moving parts — fewer modules, fewer
+abstractions, fewer configuration options, fewer lines — MUST be chosen.
+
+- Build only what a current requirement demands. Speculative generality, extension points with
+  no second caller, and configuration with no second value are prohibited.
+- Abstractions MUST be introduced in response to actual duplication or an actual requirement,
+  never in anticipation of one.
+- Any structure that adds indirection MUST be justified by the requirement it serves; if the
+  justification cannot be written in one sentence, the structure is not warranted.
+
+Rationale: in a small project the dominant cost is comprehension, not typing. Every abstraction
+is a permanent tax on every future reader, charged whether or not the flexibility is ever used.
+
+## Technology & Dependency Constraints
+
+- Language: Python, running on CPython. Minimum supported version:
+  TODO(PYTHON_VERSION) — decide and pin before the first implementation task.
+- Runtime dependencies: none. Enforced by Principle I.
+- Test dependency: `pytest`. Tests live under `tests/` and are discoverable by plain `pytest`
+  invocation from the repository root with no custom configuration required.
+- The project MUST run offline. No code path may require network access unless a specification
+  explicitly makes network access the feature under construction.
+- No build step, code generation, or compilation may be required to run the project or its
+  tests.
+
+## Development Workflow & Quality Gates
+
+A change is complete only when all of the following hold:
+
+1. Every functional requirement it implements has at least one automated test identifying that
+   requirement (Principle III).
+2. `pytest` passes in full, with no skipped tests masking unfinished work.
+3. Invalid-input behaviour has been exercised by a test and conforms to Principle II.
+4. No new runtime import outside the standard library was introduced (Principle I).
+5. No abstraction was added without a one-sentence justification tied to a current requirement
+   (Principle IV).
+
+Review expectations:
+
+- Reviewers MUST check the change against gates 1–5 above and MUST reject on any violation.
+- A violation MAY be accepted only with an explicit, recorded justification stating why no
+  compliant design meets the requirement. Unjustified complexity MUST be removed, not approved.
+- Specifications, plans, and task lists produced by Spec Kit commands are subordinate to this
+  constitution; where they conflict, this document wins and the artifact MUST be corrected.
 
 ## Governance
-<!-- Example: Constitution supersedes all other practices; Amendments require documentation, approval, migration plan -->
 
-[GOVERNANCE_RULES]
-<!-- Example: All PRs/reviews must verify compliance; Complexity must be justified; Use [GUIDANCE_FILE] for runtime development guidance -->
+This constitution supersedes all other development practices, conventions, and tooling defaults
+for this project. Where any other document, template, or habit conflicts with it, this document
+governs.
+
+**Amendment procedure**: Amendments MUST be proposed as an edit to this file, stating the
+principle or section affected, the rationale for the change, and the impact on existing code,
+specifications, and tests. An amendment takes effect only when it is approved by the project
+maintainer and merged. Amendments that invalidate existing work MUST include the migration
+required to bring that work back into compliance.
+
+**Versioning policy**: This constitution is versioned as MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH.
+
+- MAJOR: a principle or governance rule is removed, or redefined in a way that makes
+  previously compliant work non-compliant.
+- MINOR: a principle or section is added, or existing guidance is materially expanded.
+- PATCH: clarification, wording, or typo fixes that do not change what is required.
+
+**Compliance review**: Every review verifies the gates in "Development Workflow & Quality
+Gates". Accepted violations and their recorded justifications MUST be revisited at each
+amendment; a justification that no longer holds MUST be resolved by fixing the code.
 
